  "body": "If I understand [this spec](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/comms.html#the-websocket-interface:concept-websocket-establish) correctly, then messages that arrive before the `onmessage` event handler is bound in the browser will be lost.\n\nThe usual workflow for listening to WebSockets is as follows:\n\n``` javascript\nvar ws = WebSocket('wss://...');\nws.onmessage = function(event) { ... };\n```\n\nBut there appears to be no way to specify an event handler before the connection is opened.\n\nI'm working on a client library for [SaltyRTC](https://github.com/saltyrtc/saltyrtc-meta), where the protocol currently sends a \"server-hello\" message as soon as a client connects. That requires the client implementation to listen to events as soon as the connection is up.\n\nTo test this issue in the browser (latest Chromium and Firefox), I used this snippet:\n\n``` javascript\nws = new WebSocket('wss://...', 'saltyrtc-1.0'); ws.binaryType = 'arraybuffer'; setTimeout(function() { ws.onmessage = function(ev) { console.info(ev); }}, 350)\n```\n\nUp to a delay of around 350-400ms, the message is logged, but above that the message is lost.\n\nWe'd like to avoid adding an explicit client-hello message to the protocol because it is unnecessary and adds another round-trip.\n\nWhat is the usual approach to avoid the loss of messages? Is this a non-issue in real life?\n\n---\n\nAs a sidenote, it appears that in the [MessagePort spec](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/comms.html#examples-5), the connection must be opened explicitly using `.start()` or implicitly by setting the `.onmessage` handler.\n",
